import {brief} from './advanced.js';
export default [
brief('C1',1,{t:'Grammatik tanlov va muallif pozitsiyasi',goal:'Shakl o‘zgarishi sabab, mas’uliyat va ishonchni qanday ko‘rsatishini tahlil qilasiz.',rules:'Active agentni, passive jarayon yoki natijani markazga oladi; ikkalasi faktni buzmasligi kerak.|Aspect davomiylik, natija va vaqt chegarasini belgilaydi.|Grammatik shaklning ritorik ta’sirini dalil bilan izohlang, muallif niyatini taxmin bilan faktga aylantirmang.',pattern:'the committee delayed / the decision was delayed / has been reconsidering',ex:['The committee delayed the decision.','The decision was delayed pending consultation.','The committee has been reconsidering its criteria.','The criteria have been revised twice.','The wording may shift attention away from the agent.','That effect does not establish the writer’s intention.'],v:'agency|bajaruvchi roli|/ˈeɪdʒənsi/|obscure agency;stance|pozitsiya|/stɑːns/|signal a stance;implication|anglashilgan ma’no|/ˌɪmplɪˈkeɪʃən/|a possible implication;attribution|tegishlilik|/ˌætrɪˈbjuːʃən/|clear attribution',read:'Two versions of a council notice describe the same delay. The first says that the committee postponed a decision; the second says that the decision was postponed pending consultation. The passive version directs attention to the process rather than the committee. A reader might interpret this as avoidance of responsibility, but the wording alone cannot establish that intention. The notice also says that criteria have been revised twice, presenting a measurable result, whereas another sentence says that members have been reconsidering their priorities, foregrounding continuing work. A careful analysis distinguishes grammatical effect, supported inference, and speculation about motives. This makes criticism more precise: readers can request clearer attribution without claiming to know why the writer chose a particular form.',question:['Passive qanday ta’sir beradi?','it foregrounds process','it proves dishonesty','it changes the historical date','E’tibor jarayonga.'],inference:['Muallif motiviga nima yetmaydi?','independent evidence','a passive form','two versions','Shaklning o‘zi niyat dalili emas.'],keyword:'agency',responseKey:'attribution',response:'Ask for clearer attribution without pretending to know the writer’s motives.',fill:[['Criteria have been ___ twice. (revise)','revised'],['Members have been ___ priorities. (reconsider)','reconsidering'],['The wording may ___ attention. (shift)','shift']],fix:['The passive proves that the writer intended to deceive.','The passive may shift attention, but it does not prove an intention to deceive.'],transform:['Agent markazda: The decision was delayed by the committee.','The committee delayed the decision.'],task:'Ikki notice uslubini tahlil qiling: grammatik ta’sir, dalil va ehtiyotkor inference.',model:'The revised notice foregrounds the consultation process by using a passive clause. This reduces the prominence of the committee, but it does not establish an intention to avoid responsibility. The perfect simple describes two completed revisions, while the continuous form presents ongoing reconsideration. I would request a named contact and a review date. These changes would improve attribution and practical clarity without requiring a claim about motives that the text cannot substantiate.',structure:['Wording shifts attention.','Different aspects describe results and continuing work.','Separate effect from speculation about intention.']}),
brief('C1',2,{t:'Negative adverbialdan keyingi inversion',goal:'Cheklov va ta’kidni inversion bilan maqsadli ifodalaysiz.',rules:'Never/rarely/seldom/under no circumstances boshida yordamchi fe’l egadan oldin.|Not until bilan inversion asosiy gapda: Not until … did we understand.|Inversion rasmiy ta’kid; har gapni bezash uchun qo‘llamang.',pattern:'Rarely have … / Not until … did …',ex:['Rarely have we seen such agreement.','Never had the group discussed access so carefully.','Under no circumstances should names be published.','Not until the review did we understand the limitation.','Only after consultation did they revise the plan.','No sooner had we begun than the alarm sounded.'],v:'restriction|cheklov|/rɪˈstrɪkʃən/|state a restriction;emphasis|ta’kid|/ˈemfəsɪs/|place emphasis;exception|istisno|/ɪkˈsepʃən/|a rare exception;safeguard|himoya chorasi|/ˈseɪfɡɑːd/|a privacy safeguard',read:'A seminar summary opens, “Rarely have participants agreed so quickly.” The sentence creates emphasis, but the writer later reveals that only six people attended. The claim is therefore about this seminar history, not a universal pattern. A privacy note uses a stronger restriction: “Under no circumstances should names be published without permission.” Here inversion highlights an operational rule rather than an emotional reaction. The editor keeps that emphasis and simplifies a third inverted sentence because it interrupts the explanation. Advanced grammar earns its place when it supports a communicative purpose. A reader should be able to say what is being restricted, when understanding changed, or why emphasis matters. Unnecessary inversion can make ordinary information sound more dramatic than its evidence warrants.',question:['Privacy inversion vazifasi?','highlight an operational restriction','prove a universal survey result','describe six names','Qoidani ta’kidlaydi.'],inference:['Editor nega bir gapni soddalashtirdi?','its emphasis obstructed explanation','inversion is always wrong','all information was false','Maqsadsiz ta’kid o‘qishga halal.'],keyword:'restriction',responseKey:'purpose',response:'Use inversion when the communicative purpose justifies the emphasis.',fill:[['Rarely ___ we seen such agreement.','have'],['Not until the review ___ we understand.','did'],['Under no circumstances ___ names be published.','should']],fix:['Rarely we have seen such agreement.','Rarely have we seen such agreement.'],transform:['Only after boshida: They revised the plan only after consultation.','Only after consultation did they revise the plan.'],task:'Seminar summary yozing; ikkita inversion va ularning vazifasini alohida izohlang.',model:'Rarely have participants reached agreement so quickly, although the meeting involved only six people. The result should therefore be interpreted within that limited setting. Only after consultation did the committee revise the access criteria. This emphasis highlights the sequence that made the revision possible. Under no circumstances should participants’ names be published without permission. Unlike the opening observation, this final sentence states a restriction, so its strong emphasis serves a practical purpose rather than exaggerating the findings.',structure:['Emphasis needs a defined scope.','Six participants agreed at one seminar.','Keep strong wording for justified observations and rules.']}),
brief('C1',3,{t:'Cleft va pseudo-cleft',goal:'Yangi yoki tuzatiladigan ma’lumotni fokusga chiqarasiz.',rules:'It was X that … fokusni Xga qo‘yadi.|What we need is … pseudo-cleft oldingi vaziyatdan yangi talabga o‘tadi.|Ta’kid faktning doirasini o‘zgartirmasin; only ishlatilsa eksklyuziv ma’no qo‘shadi.',pattern:'It was access that … / What we need is …',ex:['It was access that concerned the students.','What we need is a clearer timetable.','It was not the price that caused the disagreement.','What the survey reveals is a gap in communication.','The reason we paused was the missing evidence.','It was only after the interview that the issue became clear.'],v:'focus|markaziy e’tibor|/ˈfəʊkəs/|shift the focus;misconception|noto‘g‘ri tasavvur|/ˌmɪskənˈsepʃən/|correct a misconception;exclusive|faqat shu|/ɪkˈskluːsɪv/|an exclusive claim;clarification|aniqlashtirish|/ˌklærɪfɪˈkeɪʃən/|request clarification',read:'A debate is described as an argument about price. The interviews show something more specific: it was access that concerned the students most. Price mattered, but travel time prevented some students from attending even a free session. The writer uses a cleft to correct the mistaken focus without claiming that price was irrelevant. She then adds, “What we need is a clearer timetable.” This pseudo-cleft moves from diagnosis to a practical request. A revised sentence saying “Only access mattered” would go further than the evidence. Focusing information is not the same as excluding every other factor. Good editing preserves that distinction and helps readers notice the central issue while retaining the complexity of the original discussion.',question:['Markaziy muammo?','access','price alone','no timetable ever existed','Travel attendancega to‘siq.'],inference:['Only access nega haddan tashqari?','price still mattered','access was irrelevant','all sessions were free','Fokus boshqa omilni rad etmaydi.'],keyword:'focus',responseKey:'complexity',response:'Correct the focus without excluding every other relevant factor.',fill:[['It was access ___ concerned them.','that'],['What we need ___ a timetable.','is'],['It was not the price ___ caused disagreement.','that']],fix:['What we need are a clearer timetable.','What we need is a clearer timetable.'],transform:['Accessga fokus: Access concerned the students.','It was access that concerned the students.'],task:'Noto‘g‘ri talqin qilingan munozarani cleft va pseudo-cleft bilan aniqlashtiring.',model:'It was access that concerned the students most. Although price was discussed, some participants could not attend even a free session because the journey was too long. What we need is a timetable that makes those constraints visible. The report should not imply that only access mattered; that would replace one oversimplification with another. A focused explanation can identify the central issue while acknowledging the factors that remain relevant to the decision.',structure:['The original summary focused on price.','Interviews identified travel constraints.','Shift the focus while preserving other factors.']}),
brief('C1',4,{t:'Fronting va information structure',goal:'Mavzudan yangi ma’lumotga o‘tishni mantiqiy tuzasiz.',rules:'Oldingi kontekstga bog‘langan bo‘lakni boshga chiqarish cohesionga xizmat qiladi.|Fronting har doim inversion talab qilmaydi: This question, we can answer.|Topic va focusni ajrating; uzun yangi ma’lumot odatda gap oxirida yengilroq o‘qiladi.',pattern:'This limitation, we can address. / Of greater concern is …',ex:['This limitation, we can address.','Of greater concern is the missing comparison group.','The first option we have already discussed.','Into the room came the organiser.','What remains uncertain is the long-term effect.','The next section examines that uncertainty.'],v:'coherence|mantiqiy yaxlitlik|/kəʊˈhɪərəns/|improve coherence;foreground|markazga chiqarish|/ˈfɔːɡraʊnd/|foreground a limitation;transition|o‘tish|/trænˈzɪʃən/|a clear transition;salient|muhim ajraluvchi|/ˈseɪliənt/|a salient point',read:'An analytical report first discusses a small sample, then unexpectedly begins a paragraph about long-term effects. Readers lose the connection because the new topic appears without a transition. The editor writes, “This limitation, we can address with a larger survey. Of greater concern is the missing comparison group.” The structure connects a known limitation to a more important uncertainty. Fronting can improve coherence, but it is not a decoration that every sentence requires. The editor also moves a long explanation to the end of a sentence so that readers meet familiar information before new detail. The revised report becomes easier to follow without reducing its complexity. Information structure helps organise attention, while evidence still determines whether the conclusions are justified.',question:['Yangi structure nima yaxshilaydi?','the connection between known and new information','the size of the sample','the evidence itself','Tashkilot dalil yaratmaydi.'],inference:['Qaysi muammo muhimroq deyiladi?','the missing comparison group','the font alone','a long sentence itself','Of greater concern.'],keyword:'transition',responseKey:'coherence',response:'Connect familiar information to the next uncertainty before adding detail.',fill:[['Of greater concern ___ the missing group.','is'],['The first option we have already ___. (discuss)','discussed'],['The next section ___ that uncertainty. (examine)','examines']],fix:['This question, can we answer. (tasdiq gap)','This question, we can answer.'],transform:['Questionni topic qiling: We can answer this question.','This question, we can answer.'],task:'Reportning uch paragrafini known→new zanjiri bilan yozing va transitionlarni belgilang.',model:'The small sample limits our confidence in the survey. This limitation, we can address by inviting a broader group of respondents. Of greater concern is the absence of a comparison group, because a larger sample alone would not explain what would have happened without the programme. The next section examines that uncertainty. Organising the information in this sequence makes the reasoning easier to follow, but it does not supply the missing evidence. The recommendation must remain proportionate to what the study actually shows.',structure:['The sample limitation is already known.','A comparison group is also missing.','Move from one uncertainty to the next with a clear transition.']}),
brief('C1',5,{t:'Conditional inversion va ehtiyot chorasi',goal:'Shartni rasmiy yozishmada ma’no saqlangan holda ifodalaysiz.',rules:'Had we known = if we had known; were it not for = if it were not for.|Should you need … kelajak ehtimoli uchun rasmiy shart.|Inverted clause yordamchini oldinga oladi, not egadan keyin; vaqt ma’nosi saqlansin.',pattern:'Had we known / Were it not for / Should you need',ex:['Had we known, we would have revised the plan.','Were it not for the grant, the project would stop.','Should you need clarification, please contact us.','Had we not checked, the error would have remained.','Were the timetable clearer, attendance might improve.','Should the trial fail, the original system will remain.'],v:'contingency|zaxira shart|/kənˈtɪndʒənsi/|a contingency plan;provision|shart bandi|/prəˈvɪʒən/|a written provision;clarification|aniqlashtirish|/ˌklærɪfɪˈkeɪʃən/|seek clarification;retain|saqlab qolmoq|/rɪˈteɪn/|retain the original system',read:'A proposal includes the provision, “Should the trial fail, the original system will remain.” This does not predict failure; it states a contingency. The review also says, “Had we known about the transport problem, we would have revised the timetable.” That clause refers to an unreal past condition. Students initially treat both sentences as future warnings because both begin with an auxiliary. The editor asks them to expand each into an if-clause and identify the time relation. Formal compression is useful only when readers can recover the intended condition. The proposal keeps the future contingency and moves the past reflection to the review section. This separates what the team will do next from what it wishes it had known earlier.',question:['Should the trial fail ma’nosi?','a future contingency','a certain failure','an unreal past fact','Should kelajak ehtimoli.'],inference:['Past reflection qayerga ko‘chdi?','the review section','the contingency list','it was deleted as false','Vaqt vazifasi ajratiladi.'],keyword:'contingency',responseKey:'time',response:'Expand the condition to check its time relation.',fill:[['___ we known, we would have revised it.','Had'],['___ you need clarification, contact us.','Should'],['Were it not ___ the grant, we would stop.','for']],fix:['Had not we checked, the error would have remained.','Had we not checked, the error would have remained.'],transform:['Inversion: If we had known, we would have revised the plan.','Had we known, we would have revised the plan.'],task:'Proposalda ikki kelajak contingency va bitta alohida o‘tmish review yozing.',model:'Should the trial fail to improve access, the original timetable will remain in place. Should participants need clarification, the organiser will provide a written explanation before the first session. These provisions describe possible future needs rather than predicting failure. In the review, we acknowledge that, had we known about the transport difficulty earlier, we would have revised the plan. Keeping that reflection separate from the contingency list helps readers distinguish an unreal past condition from a practical commitment for the next stage.',structure:['The proposal needs future contingencies.','The review concerns an unreal past condition.','Separate the two time relations.']}),
brief('C1',6,{t:'Hedging va epistemic modality',goal:'Da’vo kuchini manba va ehtimolga mos boshqarasiz.',rules:'May suggest, appears consistent with, is likely to turli ehtimol va dalil aloqalarini bildiradi.|Must xulosa kuchi; must majburiyat ham bo‘lishi mumkin — kontekstni tekshiring.|Hedging noaniqlikni yashirmasin: aynan nima noma’lum va nima aniq ekanini ayting.',pattern:'may suggest / appears consistent with / cannot establish',ex:['The findings may suggest a trend.','The result appears consistent with our prediction.','The data cannot establish causation.','The response is likely to depend on context.','This explanation must be treated cautiously.','A larger study would be needed to confirm the pattern.'],v:'causation|sababiy aloqa|/kɔːˈzeɪʃən/|establish causation;correlation|birga o‘zgarish|/ˌkɒrəˈleɪʃən/|observe a correlation;plausibility|ishonarlilik|/ˌplɔːzəˈbɪləti/|assess plausibility;qualified|cheklangan shartli|/ˈkwɒlɪfaɪd/|a qualified conclusion',read:'Students who attended the workshop reported greater confidence afterwards. The report says that the workshop caused the improvement. However, the students volunteered to attend, and no comparison group was observed. The result is consistent with a positive effect, but other explanations remain plausible: motivated students may practise more outside the workshop. A qualified conclusion therefore states the observed change and the uncertainty about its cause separately. This is not excessive caution. It prevents a recommendation from being presented as a demonstrated mechanism. The organiser can still propose another trial, provided the design collects evidence that distinguishes competing explanations. Good modality allows a clear decision while preserving the limits of knowledge.',question:['Causation nega aniqlanmagan?','no comparison and self-selection','confidence never changed','all volunteers are identical','Muqobil sabablar bor.'],inference:['Hedging qarorni to‘xtatadimi?','not necessarily','always','it proves the mechanism','Sinov tavsiyasi davom etishi mumkin.'],keyword:'causation',responseKey:'uncertainty',response:'Recommend a better trial without claiming that causation has been established.',fill:[['The findings may ___ a trend. (suggest)','suggest'],['The data cannot ___ causation. (establish)','establish'],['A study would be ___ to confirm it. (need)','needed']],fix:['The correlation proves that the workshop caused improvement.','The correlation is consistent with improvement, but does not establish causation.'],transform:['Da’voni cheklang: The workshop caused improvement.','The findings may suggest that the workshop contributed to improvement.'],task:'Survey sharhini observed change, plausible mechanism va noma’lum qism bilan yozing.',model:'Participants reported greater confidence after the workshop. This result is consistent with a positive effect, but the study cannot establish causation because attendance was voluntary and no comparison group was observed. Motivation and additional practice remain plausible explanations. I recommend a further trial that records those factors and compares similar groups. The current evidence supports continued investigation rather than a claim that the mechanism has been demonstrated. Stating that limitation makes the recommendation more precise, not less useful.',structure:['Confidence increased among participants.','Selection and outside practice were not controlled.','Recommend further investigation with a qualified conclusion.']}),
brief('C1',7,{t:'Murakkab reporting va passive',goal:'Vaqt, manba va dalil kuchini bitta attributionda saqlaysiz.',rules:'Is believed to have been changed oldingi passive hodisaga ishora qiladi.|It has been suggested that … taklif, established fact emas.|Bir manbani ko‘p sayt takrorlashi mustaqil tasdiq bo‘lmaydi.',pattern:'is believed to have been / has been suggested that',ex:['The policy is believed to have been revised.','It has been suggested that access improved.','The report is understood to be preliminary.','The figures are said to have been checked.','The claim has not been independently confirmed.','The original source should be identified.'],v:'independent|mustaqil|/ˌɪndɪˈpendənt/|independent confirmation;preliminary|dastlabki|/prɪˈlɪmɪnəri/|a preliminary report;circulate|tarqalmoq|/ˈsɜːkjəleɪt/|circulate a claim;trace|izini topmoq|/treɪs/|trace a source',read:'Several posts state that the access policy is believed to have been revised. The wording sounds cautious, yet none identifies who believes it or what revision occurred. The student researcher traces every post to the same preliminary report. Repetition has created an appearance of confirmation without adding an independent source. The original report says only that a revision was proposed. The researcher rewrites the summary to name the report and distinguish proposal from implementation. Complex reporting forms can preserve useful time relations, but they can also conceal missing attribution. A reliable analysis asks who made the claim, which event is described, and whether the source supports the stronger version now circulating.',question:['Ko‘p posts nega tasdiq emas?','they repeat one preliminary source','they use different fonts','all proposals are implemented','Mustaqil dalil qo‘shilmagan.'],inference:['Original report nima dedi?','a revision was proposed','a revision was completed','the policy never existed','Proposal amalga oshirish emas.'],keyword:'source',responseKey:'independence',response:'Trace the original source before treating repeated reports as confirmation.',fill:[['The policy is believed to have been ___. (revise)','revised'],['The report is ___ to be preliminary. (understand)','understood'],['The claim has not been ___ confirmed. (independent)','independently']],fix:['Several copies of one report provide independent confirmation.','Several copies of one report do not provide independent confirmation.'],transform:['Oldingi passive reporting: People believe someone revised the policy.','The policy is believed to have been revised.'],task:'Source audit yozing: attribution, time relation va claimning kuchaygan joyi.',model:'The circulating posts all derive from one preliminary report. That report proposed a revision; it did not confirm implementation. The policy is therefore not known to have been revised. Repetition across several pages should not be counted as independent confirmation. Our summary should name the original source and preserve its status as a proposal. Complex reporting language is useful when it clarifies attribution and time, but the wording should not make unsupported information appear more established than it is.',structure:['The claim appears in several posts.','All posts trace to one preliminary proposal.','Repeated attribution is not independent confirmation.']}),
brief('C1',8,{t:'Participle clauses: sabab va ehtiyotkor talqin',goal:'Qisqartirilgan sababiy aloqani egasi va dalili bilan tekshirasiz.',rules:'Having + V3 oldin tugagan amal; -ing bir vaqtdagi yoki sababiy aloqa bo‘lishi mumkin.|Ega asosiy gap bilan mos; passive uchun past participle.|Participle sababni taxminan berishi mumkin; as/because bilan kengaytirib dalilni tekshiring.',pattern:'Having reviewed … / Concerned about … / Assuming that …',ex:['Having reviewed the evidence, we revised the claim.','Concerned about access, the committee requested a trial.','Assuming that attendance remains stable, the plan is feasible.','Written for specialists, the report needs explanation.','Not having compared groups, we cannot identify the cause.','Working with limited data, the team stated its uncertainty.'],v:'premise|asosiy faraz|/ˈpremɪs/|test a premise;implicit|yashirin|/ɪmˈplɪsɪt/|an implicit assumption;feasibility|amalga oshish imkoniyati|/ˌfiːzəˈbɪləti/|assess feasibility;qualify|cheklab ifodalamoq|/ˈkwɒlɪfaɪ/|qualify a claim',read:'A proposal says, “Assuming that attendance remains stable, the plan is feasible.” The assumption is essential: if attendance falls, the cost per participant may rise. A shortened clause makes that condition compact, but readers must not lose it when quoting the recommendation. Another sentence says, “Having reviewed the evidence, the claim was revised.” Its implied subject is the claim, which cannot review evidence. The writer names the team and checks whether the review actually preceded the revision. Editing therefore involves more than selecting an elegant participle. It tests the subject, the time relation, and any premise compressed into the clause. These checks prevent concise prose from quietly strengthening a conclusion.',question:['Feasibility nimaga bog‘liq?','stable attendance','every cost being zero','no assumptions','Essential premise.'],inference:['Claim reviewed xatosi?','the implied subject cannot review evidence','the review happened later necessarily','all passive is wrong','Ega claim emas team.'],keyword:'premise',responseKey:'qualification',response:'Keep the assumption visible when quoting the recommendation.',fill:[['Having ___ the evidence, we revised it. (review)','reviewed'],['___ for specialists, the report needs explanation. (write)','Written'],['Not ___ compared groups, we cannot identify cause.','having']],fix:['Having reviewed the evidence, the claim was revised.','Having reviewed the evidence, the team revised the claim.'],transform:['Avvalgi amal bilan: We reviewed the evidence, then revised the claim.','Having reviewed the evidence, we revised the claim.'],task:'Proposalni uch participle clause bilan yozib, yashirin premise va egani tekshiring.',model:'Having reviewed the attendance figures, the team considers the plan feasible. This conclusion assumes that participation remains broadly stable; a substantial fall would change the cost per participant. Written for the committee, the report should make that premise visible rather than bury it in a compact clause. Not having compared alternative schedules, we cannot claim that this arrangement is the most efficient. We can recommend a limited trial while keeping the conditions of that recommendation explicit.',structure:['The recommendation depends on an assumption.','Lower attendance would change cost per participant.','Preserve the premise in the concise summary.']}),
brief('C1',9,{t:'Nominalisation: jarayon va bajaruvchi',goal:'Otlashtirishni aniqlik uchun ishlatasiz va yashirilgan agentni qayta ochasiz.',rules:'Implement → implementation; decide → decision jarayonni noun qiladi.|Nominalisation matnni ixcham bog‘laydi, ammo agent va vaqtni yashirishi mumkin.|It led to the implementation of … o‘rniga kim nima qilganini yozish ba’zan aniqroq.',pattern:'implementation / evaluation / allocation',ex:['The committee implemented the plan.','Implementation began in May.','The allocation of rooms was reviewed.','The review identified a communication gap.','The organiser allocated two rooms.','Evaluation should include access as well as cost.'],v:'implementation|amalga oshirish|/ˌɪmplɪmenˈteɪʃən/|monitor implementation;allocation|taqsimlash|/ˌæləˈkeɪʃən/|room allocation;evaluation|baholash|/ɪˌvæljuˈeɪʃən/|an independent evaluation;accountability|hisobdorlik|/əˌkaʊntəˈbɪləti/|maintain accountability',read:'The report states that the allocation of rooms resulted in the exclusion of one group. Readers can identify the outcome but not who allocated the rooms or whether the exclusion was intended. The writer expands the sentence: the organiser assigned both rooms to early applicants, leaving later applicants without space. This version names the agent and explains the sequence without guessing intent. Later, the report uses “the allocation” to refer back to that process efficiently. Nominalisation is therefore not inherently evasive. It can maintain cohesion once the action is clear. Problems arise when abstract nouns replace necessary information about who acted, when, and under which criteria. A good revision alternates explicit actions with concise references to them.',question:['Expanded sentence nimani qo‘shdi?','agent and sequence','proof of intent','a new outcome','Kim taqsimlagani va tartib.'],inference:['Nominalisation har doim evasivmi?','No, it can link known actions','Yes, always','only in short words','Ma’lum jarayonga ixcham qaytish.'],keyword:'allocation',responseKey:'accountability',response:'Name the action before using an abstract noun to refer back to it.',fill:[['Implement → noun: ___','implementation'],['Evaluate → noun: ___','evaluation'],['Allocate → noun: ___','allocation']],fix:['The exclusion proves the organiser intended harm.','The allocation excluded a group, but does not establish the organiser’s intention.'],transform:['Agentni oching: Room allocation was carried out by the organiser.','The organiser allocated the rooms.'],task:'Bitta abstract reportni agentli gaplarga oching, keyin cohesion uchun otlashtiring.',model:'The organiser allocated both rooms to early applicants, leaving later groups without space. This allocation followed the order of application, but the report does not show whether the criteria had been announced clearly. Evaluation should therefore examine communication as well as the outcome. Using the noun “allocation” helps refer back to the process once the agent and sequence are explicit. It should not replace the information readers need to understand responsibility or imply an intention the evidence does not establish.',structure:['An abstract noun hides the agent.','The expanded sentence names the organiser and sequence.','Use nominalisation after the action becomes clear.']}),
brief('C1',10,{t:'Ellipsis va substitution',goal:'Takrorni kamaytirib, yashirin ma’no manbasini aniq saqlaysiz.',rules:'So/neither yordamchi bilan: So do I; neither did she.|Do so oldingi amal, one/ones ot, so/not clause mazmunini almashtirishi mumkin.|Ellipsis kontekstdan tiklanadigan qismni tashlaydi; turli vazifalarni noto‘g‘ri tenglashtirmang.',pattern:'do so / one / I think so / neither did',ex:['We tested the first plan, and they did so too.','The cheaper option is not always the better one.','Will the trial continue? I think so.','I did not agree, and neither did she.','Some preferred mornings; others, afternoons.','The team could revise the plan, but chose not to.'],v:'referent|ishora qilingan birlik|/ˈrefərənt/|a clear referent;recover|tiklamoq|/rɪˈkʌvə/|recover omitted meaning;parallel|parallel mos|/ˈpærəlel/|a parallel structure;substitution|almashtirish|/ˌsʌbstɪˈtjuːʃən/|use substitution',read:'A summary says that one group tested the plan and another did so too. Readers naturally infer that the groups performed comparable tests. In fact, the second group only discussed the plan. The substitution hides a meaningful difference, so the editor restores the full verb. Elsewhere, “some preferred mornings; others, afternoons” is concise and accurate because the missing verb is shared. Ellipsis works when the omitted material can be recovered without changing the claim. The same principle applies to “the better one”: readers need to know which options are being compared and by which criterion. Advanced cohesion is not simply fewer repeated words. It is economical wording that preserves the distinctions necessary for fair interpretation.',question:['Did so nimani noto‘g‘ri anglatdi?','comparable testing','different discussion only','no group acted','Aslida second faqat muhokama.'],inference:['Qaysi ellipsis asoslangan?','some preferred mornings; others, afternoons','tested and discussed are identical','every option is the better one','Shared verb bir xil.'],keyword:'referent',responseKey:'distinction',response:'Substitution must preserve the distinction between testing and discussing.',fill:[['They tested it, and we did ___ too.','so'],['I did not agree; neither ___ she.','did'],['The better ___ is unclear. (option o‘rnida)','one']],fix:['We discussed it, and they tested it, so both groups did the same thing.','One group discussed it, while the other tested it.'],transform:['Ellipsis: Some preferred mornings; others preferred afternoons.','Some preferred mornings; others, afternoons.'],task:'Summaryda substitution qo‘llang, har referent va omitted materialni izohlang.',model:'The first group tested the timetable, while the second discussed it. I have kept both verbs because “did so too” would imply comparable activity. Some participants preferred mornings; others, afternoons. Here the omitted verb is shared and easily recovered. The cheaper option may be useful, but it is not necessarily the better one for access. That final substitution remains clear only if the reader knows the options and the criterion. Concision should preserve meaningful distinctions rather than conceal them.',structure:['One group tested; another discussed.','Substitution would imply the same activity.','Restore the verbs where the distinction matters.']}),
brief('C1',11,{t:'Complex relative va noun clauses',goal:'What, whoever, whose va proposition clauses bilan aniq chegaralar berasiz.',rules:'What = the thing that; the thing what standard shakl emas.|Whoever shaxsni umumiy tanlaydi; whose egalikni, which oldingi clausega ishora qilishi mumkin.|That-clause propositionni beradi; whether muqobil/noaniqlikni saqlaydi.',pattern:'what matters / whether … / whoever … / which …',ex:['What matters is whether the plan improves access.','Whoever leads the trial must record attendance.','The group whose proposal was accepted will present.','The fact that attendance rose needs explanation.','They revised the plan, which reduced confusion.','We cannot assume that every participant benefited.'],v:'proposition|ifodalangan da’vo|/ˌprɒpəˈzɪʃən/|evaluate a proposition;scope|qamrov|/skəʊp/|define the scope;antecedent|oldingi tayanch birlik|/ˌæntɪˈsiːdənt/|a clear antecedent;beneficiary|foyda oluvchi|/ˌbenɪˈfɪʃəri/|identify beneficiaries',read:'The committee agrees that what matters is whether the new schedule improves access. Attendance rose, but that fact alone does not show who benefited. The group whose proposal was accepted reports that more students came on the first day. Another group asks whether distant students attended more often or whether nearby students simply returned twice. Both interpretations fit the total. A noun clause lets the writer state the uncertainty rather than convert it into a conclusion. The summary also uses “which reduced confusion” to refer to the revision of the notice, not to the committee itself. Clear clause relations define the scope of claims and help readers locate what still needs investigation.',question:['Attendance total nimani ko‘rsatmaydi?','who benefited','that attendance rose','a reported total','Kimlar o‘zgargani noma’lum.'],inference:['Whether nima saqlaydi?','alternative interpretations','a guaranteed benefit','a named leader only','Ikki talqin ochiq.'],keyword:'scope',responseKey:'beneficiaries',response:'A higher total does not identify the beneficiaries.',fill:[['___ matters is whether access improves.','What'],['The group ___ proposal was accepted will present.','whose'],['We ask ___ distant students benefited.','whether']],fix:['The thing what matters is access.','What matters is access.'],transform:['Noun clause bilan: Did the plan help distant students? This is uncertain.','Whether the plan helped distant students is uncertain.'],task:'Reportda what-matters clause, whether va whole-clause which bilan uncertainty aniqlang.',model:'What matters is whether the schedule improves access for students who previously struggled to attend. The fact that total attendance rose is encouraging, but it does not identify the beneficiaries. The group whose proposal was accepted should compare participation by relevant groups rather than rely on the total alone. The notice was revised to clarify the schedule, which reduced confusion. That improvement is distinct from the unresolved question of access. Clear clause relations help keep those two findings separate.',structure:['The total rose.','The beneficiaries remain unidentified.','Investigate who gained access rather than infer from the total.']}),
brief('C1',12,{t:'Discourse markers va argument yo‘li',goal:'Indeed, nevertheless, conversely va consequentlyni real aloqa uchun tanlaysiz.',rules:'Nevertheless — kutilgan xulosaga qarshi; consequently — sababdan oqibat.|Conversely teskari aloqa; moreover faqat yangi mos dalil qo‘shadi.|Marker argument yaratmaydi: har aloqani markersiz oddiy gap bilan izohlab tekshiring.',pattern:'claim → nevertheless → qualification / consequently → result',ex:['The result is encouraging; nevertheless, the sample is limited.','The room was closed; consequently, the meeting moved.','Some prefer speed; conversely, others prioritise depth.','The survey is small; moreover, it excludes evening users.','Indeed, the interviews reveal a broader concern.','On balance, a further trial is justified.'],v:'concession|qarshi hisobga olish|/kənˈseʃən/|signal concession;consequence|oqibat|/ˈkɒnsɪkwəns/|signal consequence;addition|qo‘shimcha|/əˈdɪʃən/|a relevant addition;inference|anglangan xulosa|/ˈɪnfərəns/|a justified inference',read:'A draft links every paragraph with “therefore.” It moves from a small survey to a recommendation, then from an access concern to a budget figure. These are not all causal consequences. The editor replaces one marker with “nevertheless” because the limitation qualifies an encouraging result. Another becomes “moreover” because the budget concern adds a different reason for caution. The recommendation remains the writer’s judgement, not an inevitable logical result. Readers need a transparent path through the argument. Discourse markers can reveal that path when chosen accurately, but they can also pretend that an unsupported jump is a deduction. The editor tests every connection by explaining it in ordinary language before selecting the marker.',question:['Thereforelar muammosi?','not all connections were consequences','all paragraphs were identical','therefore is never valid','Aloqalar turli.'],inference:['Recommendation qanday maqomda?','a reasoned judgement','an inevitable deduction','a survey response','Dalilga tayangan baho, majburiy natija emas.'],keyword:'inference',responseKey:'connection',response:'Explain the connection before choosing its marker.',fill:[['The result is good; ___, the sample is limited.','nevertheless'],['The room closed; ___, we moved.','consequently'],['The sample is small; ___, it excludes evening users.','moreover']],fix:['The sample is limited; therefore, the positive result is certainly universal.','The result is positive; nevertheless, the sample is limited.'],transform:['Concession bilan: The result is encouraging, but the sample is limited.','The result is encouraging; nevertheless, the sample is limited.'],task:'Argument yo‘lini kamida to‘rt turdagi marker bilan tuzing va har aloqa vazifasini yozing.',model:'The survey result is encouraging; nevertheless, the small sample limits its scope. Moreover, evening users were excluded, so the findings do not represent every group. The interviews raise a separate access concern rather than a direct consequence of the survey. On balance, a further trial is justified, provided it collects broader evidence. This recommendation is a judgement about the available information. The markers should make the reasoning visible without presenting that judgement as an inevitable deduction.',structure:['Evidence and limitation coexist.','An additional group was excluded.','A further trial is a qualified judgement.']}),
brief('C1',13,{t:'Cohesion: reference va lexical chains',goal:'Matn bo‘ylab fikr zanjirini pronoun, sinonim va aniq takror bilan saqlaysiz.',rules:'This/it/they referenti yaqin va aniq bo‘lsin.|Sinonimni takror uchun ishlatish tushunchani o‘zgartirmasin: attendance participationga har doim teng emas.|Topic chain va reference xaritasini tekshirib, muhim atamani kerak joyda takrorlang.',pattern:'attendance → this increase / the intervention → it',ex:['Attendance rose, but this increase needs explanation.','Participation also depends on meaningful involvement.','The trial introduced a new timetable.','This intervention was reviewed after two weeks.','The timetable, rather than the survey, caused confusion.','Repeating the term can improve precision.'],v:'cohesion|matn bog‘liqligi|/kəʊˈhiːʒən/|maintain cohesion;referent|tayanch birlik|/ˈrefərənt/|resolve a referent;engagement|faol ishtirok|/ɪnˈɡeɪdʒmənt/|meaningful engagement;precision|aniqlik|/prɪˈsɪʒən/|lexical precision',read:'A report says that attendance rose and then describes this as greater engagement. The terms are related but not identical: being present does not show how actively someone participated. Later, “this caused confusion” could refer either to the timetable or to the survey mentioned in the previous sentence. The editor repeats the timetable explicitly and keeps attendance as the term for the measured count. She uses engagement only when discussing the separate interview evidence. Avoiding repetition is not worth introducing a different construct or an ambiguous pronoun. A cohesive text gives readers a reliable chain of ideas. That chain can include deliberate repetition, clear reference, and carefully chosen lexical variation rather than a different synonym in every sentence.',question:['Attendance engagementga tengmi?','not necessarily','always','neither can be measured','Presence active contribution emas.'],inference:['Editor nega timetable takrorladi?','to resolve an ambiguous reference','to hide the survey','to increase word count','This qaysi obyektga noma’lum.'],keyword:'cohesion',responseKey:'precision',response:'Repeat a term when variation would change the measured concept.',fill:[['Attendance rose; this ___ needs explanation. (increase)','increase'],['Participation depends ___ involvement.','on'],['This intervention was ___ after two weeks. (review)','reviewed']],fix:['Attendance rose, so meaningful engagement definitely rose.','Attendance rose, but meaningful engagement requires separate evidence.'],transform:['Referentni oching: This caused confusion. (timetable nazarda)','The timetable caused confusion.'],task:'Reportda reference chain yarating; attendance va engagement uchun alohida dalil bering.',model:'Attendance increased during the trial. This increase refers to the number of people present, rather than the quality of their involvement. Interviews provide separate evidence of engagement, but they cover only a small group. The timetable caused confusion during the first week; naming it explicitly avoids an ambiguous “this.” In the revision, I retain key terms when a synonym would change the construct being measured. Cohesion should help readers follow precise ideas, not merely prevent repeated words.',structure:['Attendance and engagement are related but distinct.','Counts and interviews provide different evidence.','Use a precise reference chain for each construct.']}),
brief('C1',14,{t:'Register va tone: qat’iy, hurmatli yozishma',goal:'Professional e’tirozni auditoriya va munosabatga mos boshqarasiz.',rules:'Tone maqsad, munosabat va so‘ralgan amal bilan mos.|We would welcome clarification qat’iy savolni hurmatli qiladi; passiv agressiv taxmindan saqlaning.|Formal register uzunlik emas: aniq noun/verb va chegaralangan claim tanlang.',pattern:'We would welcome clarification / The evidence does not support …',ex:['We would welcome clarification of the criteria.','The evidence does not support that interpretation.','We recognise the constraints on the team.','Nevertheless, the missing information affects our decision.','Could you specify the basis for the estimate?','We propose a brief meeting to resolve the issue.'],v:'diplomatic|murosa ohangli|/ˌdɪpləˈmætɪk/|a diplomatic objection;constraint|cheklov|/kənˈstreɪnt/|recognise constraints;basis|asos|/ˈbeɪsɪs/|the basis of an estimate;resolve|hal qilmoq|/rɪˈzɒlv/|resolve a disagreement',read:'A committee email begins, “As you obviously know, your estimate is wrong.” The writer wants clarification, but the opening attributes knowledge and fault without establishing either. A revised version recognises the team’s constraints, identifies the unsupported estimate, and asks for its basis. The objection remains firm: the missing information affects whether the committee can approve the plan. Diplomatic tone does not require pretending to agree. It separates criticism of a claim from speculation about the person making it. The editor also removes elaborate phrases that obscure the request. A professional recipient should be able to identify what is disputed, what evidence would address it, and which action is proposed next.',question:['Birinchi opening muammosi?','unsupported attribution of knowledge and fault','it asks too clearly','it names constraints','Motiv/kengaygan ayb.'],inference:['Firmness qanday saqlanadi?','state how missing evidence affects approval','use stronger insults','pretend agreement','Amaliy qaror ta’siri.'],keyword:'basis',responseKey:'clarification',response:'Challenge the estimate while asking for evidence that could resolve the issue.',fill:[['We would ___ clarification. (welcome)','welcome'],['Could you specify the ___ of the estimate?','basis'],['We propose a meeting ___ resolve it.','to']],fix:['As you obviously know, your estimate is wrong.','The evidence does not support the estimate; could you clarify its basis?'],transform:['Diplomatic so‘rov: Explain your estimate.','Could you specify the basis for the estimate?'],task:'Disputed estimate bo‘yicha professional email: firm objection, evidence request va next action.',model:'Dear project team, we recognise the constraints involved in preparing the plan. Nevertheless, the estimate currently lacks a clear basis, and this affects whether the committee can approve the proposal. Could you specify which attendance figures and staffing assumptions were used? We would welcome clarification rather than a revised total without supporting detail. A brief meeting could help resolve the issue before Friday. Our objection concerns the evidence behind the estimate, not the team’s motives or commitment.',structure:['The estimate lacks a stated basis.','Approval depends on that missing information.','Request evidence and propose a practical discussion.']}),
brief('C1',15,{t:'Precise vocabulary va lexical bundles',goal:'Nozik ma’no farqini va dalilga mos collocationni tanlaysiz.',rules:'Viable, efficient, equitable turli mezonlar; barchasini good deb almashtirmang.|In light of, to the extent that, a matter of lexical bundles munosabatni aniq beradi.|Aniq so‘z kamyob so‘z degani emas; kontekst va collocationni tekshiring.',pattern:'viable / efficient / equitable / in light of the evidence',ex:['The plan is viable with current staffing.','It is efficient in terms of time.','It may not be equitable for distant students.','In light of the evidence, we recommend revision.','To the extent that access improves, the trial is useful.','The issue is a matter of distribution, not total capacity.'],v:'viable|amalga oshadigan|/ˈvaɪəbəl/|a viable plan;efficient|resursni tejamli ishlatadigan|/ɪˈfɪʃənt/|an efficient process;equitable|adolatli taqsimlangan|/ˈekwɪtəbəl/|equitable access;distribution|taqsimot|/ˌdɪstrɪˈbjuːʃən/|resource distribution',read:'The proposal is described as good, but committee members mean different things by that word. The organiser considers it viable because existing staff can run it. The finance group calls it efficient because it uses fewer hours per participant. Students ask whether it is equitable, since those living far away may still be excluded. No single adjective answers all three questions. The report adopts the separate terms and explains the criteria behind them. In light of the evidence, the committee supports a revised trial rather than a blanket endorsement. Precise vocabulary does not make the text more impressive by sounding unusual. It allows readers to see which judgement is being made, which evidence supports it, and where the judgements may conflict.',question:['Equitable qanday mezon?','fair distribution of access','fewest staff hours only','technical feasibility only','Adolatli access.'],inference:['Separate terms nima uchun?','the judgements can conflict','rare words are always better','all mean identical success','Uch mezon bir xil emas.'],keyword:'equitable',responseKey:'criteria',response:'State the criterion before judging whether the plan is good.',fill:[['In ___ of the evidence, revise it.','light'],['The plan is ___ with current staffing. (amalga oshadi)','viable'],['Access should be fairly distributed: ___.','equitable']],fix:['Efficient automatically means equitable.','An efficient plan is not necessarily equitable.'],transform:['Aniq mezon: The plan is good because current staff can run it.','The plan is viable with current staffing.'],task:'Proposalni viable/efficient/equitable mezonlarda alohida baholang.',model:'The plan appears viable with current staffing and efficient in its use of time. However, it may not be equitable for students who travel far. These judgements use different criteria and should not be combined into a general claim that the plan is good. In light of the evidence, I recommend revising the schedule and measuring participation by relevant groups. The aim is to preserve feasibility while testing whether access becomes more fairly distributed. Precise vocabulary makes the trade-off visible.',structure:['Good hides several criteria.','Feasibility, efficiency, and equity differ.','Evaluate each criterion with matching evidence.']}),
brief('C1',16,{t:'Idiomlar: uslub va kontekst',goal:'Idiomni tushunasiz, lekin noaniq yoki nomos joyda literal ma’noni tanlaysiz.',rules:'On the same page, a grey area, take with a pinch of salt kontekstda ma’no oladi.|Idiom literal tarjima bilan ochilmasligi mumkin; auditoriyaga mosligini tekshiring.|Professional safety yoki aniq yo‘riqnomada figurative ibora o‘rniga oddiy aniq gap afzal.',pattern:'on the same page / a grey area / take with a pinch of salt',ex:['We need to be on the same page about the criteria.','The policy leaves a grey area.','Take the unofficial estimate with a pinch of salt.','Let us spell out the conditions.','The discussion went back to square one.','For new readers, state the meaning directly.'],v:'figurative|ko‘chma|/ˈfɪɡərətɪv/|figurative language;literal|to‘g‘ridan-to‘g‘ri|/ˈlɪtərəl/|a literal interpretation;ambiguity|noaniqlik|/ˌæmbɪˈɡjuːəti/|avoid ambiguity;idiom|turg‘un ibora|/ˈɪdiəm/|a context-appropriate idiom',read:'A meeting leader says the team must be on the same page. Experienced members understand that they need shared criteria, but a new participant thinks they should open a document. The misunderstanding is harmless here, yet it shows why audience matters. Another note calls the rules a grey area without specifying what is unclear. The editor replaces the phrase with an exact description of the missing condition. Idioms can create a natural conversational tone and express attitudes efficiently. They are less useful when precision or accessibility is the main priority. Advanced language use includes knowing when not to use an idiom. The revised guide states conditions directly and leaves figurative expressions for discussion where clarification can be requested.',question:['New participant nega chalkashdi?','a literal interpretation','a wrong document page','no criteria existed','Ko‘chma ma’no yangi.'],inference:['Advanced use nimani ham o‘z ichiga oladi?','choosing not to use an idiom','using idioms everywhere','never speaking naturally','Auditoriya/aniqlik mezoni.'],keyword:'literal',responseKey:'audience',response:'Use direct wording when readers need exact conditions.',fill:[['Be on the same ___.','page'],['Take it with a pinch of ___.','salt'],['Spell ___ the conditions.','out']],fix:['A grey area tells readers exactly which condition is missing.','A grey area signals uncertainty but does not specify the missing condition.'],transform:['Literal yozing: We need to be on the same page about the criteria.','We need to agree on the criteria.'],task:'Meeting dialogda idiom ishlating, keyin yangi a’zo uchun plain-language guide yozing.',model:'In discussion, “on the same page” can signal the need for shared understanding. In the written guide, I would instead say that members must agree on the criteria before judging proposals. Calling the policy a grey area is also insufficient unless the missing condition is named. The revised wording explains that no deadline has been specified. This is less figurative but more accessible. Choosing direct language does not reduce sophistication; it aligns the wording with the reader’s task.',structure:['An idiom has been interpreted literally.','The guide needs exact conditions.','Choose direct language for accessible instructions.']}),
brief('C1',17,{t:'Paraphrasing: ma’no chegarasi',goal:'Manba da’vosini kuchaytirmasdan boshqa so‘zlarda ifodalaysiz.',rules:'Paraphrase mazmun, scope, attribution va certaintyni saqlaydi.|Synonym almashtirishning o‘zi yetmaydi; structure o‘zgarishi mumkin, claim emas.|May contribute to → causes ga o‘tish noto‘g‘ri kuchaytirish; source reference qoladi.',pattern:'may contribute → could play a part / some → a subset',ex:['The programme may contribute to confidence.','The programme could play a part in greater confidence.','Some participants reported a benefit.','A subset of participants described an improvement.','The report does not establish a cause.','The paraphrase must preserve that limitation.'],v:'fidelity|mazmunga sodiqlik|/fɪˈdeləti/|semantic fidelity;distortion|mazmun buzilishi|/dɪˈstɔːʃən/|avoid distortion;qualification|cheklov|/ˌkwɒlɪfɪˈkeɪʃən/|retain a qualification;acknowledge|tan olib ko‘rsatmoq|/əkˈnɒlɪdʒ/|acknowledge a source',read:'The source says that the programme may contribute to confidence among some participants. A draft paraphrase says that it makes students confident. The shorter wording removes three qualifications: possibility, contribution rather than sole causation, and a limited group. A faithful paraphrase can change structure and vocabulary while retaining those boundaries. The editor writes that the programme could play a part in greater confidence for a subset of participants. She also attributes the claim to the report rather than presenting it as her own established finding. The purpose is not to preserve every word. It is to preserve the claim the words make. Checking scope and certainty is therefore as important as avoiding copied phrasing.',question:['Draft nima yo‘qotdi?','possibility, contribution, and limited scope','only spelling','the programme name only','Uch qualification.'],inference:['Faithful paraphrase nima saqlaydi?','claim boundaries and attribution','every original word','only sentence length','Ma’no/ishonch/qamrov.'],keyword:'qualification',responseKey:'fidelity',response:'Change the wording while retaining the limits of the claim.',fill:[['The programme may ___ to confidence. (contribute)','contribute'],['It could play a ___ in improvement.','part'],['A ___ of participants reported benefit. (kichik qism)','subset']],fix:['Some participants may benefit means all students definitely benefit.','Some participants may benefit does not establish that all students definitely benefit.'],transform:['Faithful paraphrase: The programme may contribute to confidence.','The programme could play a part in greater confidence.'],task:'Uch source gapni paraphrase qiling; scope, certainty va attribution jadvali tuzing.',model:'According to the report, the programme could play a part in greater confidence for a subset of participants. This paraphrase retains the original possibility and does not turn contribution into sole causation. It also preserves the limited scope rather than referring to all students. The report’s claim remains attributed to its source. In revision, I check those boundaries before choosing alternative words. A shorter sentence is not a faithful paraphrase if it removes the qualifications that define the original meaning.',structure:['The source contains three limitations.','The draft removes them.','Restore scope, certainty, and attribution.']}),
brief('C1',18,{t:'Summarising va synthesis',goal:'Ikki manbaning mos va ziddiyatli qismlarini xolis birlashtirasiz.',rules:'Summary bitta manbaning asosiy mazmuni; synthesis manbalar o‘rtasidagi aloqani tuzadi.|Turli population va method natijalari yuzaki qarama-qarshi ko‘rinishi mumkin.|Har claimni manbasiga bog‘lang, mos kelmagan qismni yashirmang.',pattern:'Source A … whereas Source B … / taken together …',ex:['The survey reports a general preference.','The interviews describe individual constraints.','These findings are not necessarily contradictory.','Taken together, they suggest a mixed approach.','Neither source measures long-term attendance.','A synthesis should retain that shared limitation.'],v:'synthesis|birlashtiruvchi tahlil|/ˈsɪnθəsɪs/|develop a synthesis;converge|moslashmoq|/kənˈvɜːdʒ/|findings converge;diverge|farqlashmoq|/daɪˈvɜːdʒ/|findings diverge;methodology|tadqiqot usuli|/ˌmeθəˈdɒlədʒi/|compare methodology',read:'Source A is a survey of sixty students that favours a fixed afternoon session. Source B contains interviews with six students who travel far; they describe difficulties attending on that day. Calling the sources contradictory overlooks the different questions they answer. The survey records preference across a wider group, while the interviews explain constraints for a particular group. Taken together, the sources support testing a mixed schedule, but neither measures long-term attendance. A synthesis should not choose one source simply because it has more respondents or more detailed stories. It compares scope, method, and relevance to the decision. The conclusion can integrate both forms of evidence while keeping the shared limitation visible.',question:['Sources nega shartli zid emas?','they answer different questions','both have sixty people','interviews measure all attendance','Population va maqsad boshqa.'],inference:['Ikkalasining cheklovi?','no long-term attendance measure','no student responses','no preference evidence','Shared limitation.'],keyword:'methodology',responseKey:'synthesis',response:'Compare scope and method instead of ranking sources by size alone.',fill:[['Taken ___, they suggest a mixed approach.','together'],['Neither source ___ long-term attendance. (measure)','measures'],['The findings are not necessarily ___. (contradictory)','contradictory']],fix:['The larger survey automatically makes the interviews irrelevant.','The larger survey and the interviews provide different relevant evidence.'],transform:['Integrate: The survey measures preference. The interviews explain constraints.','The survey measures preference, whereas the interviews explain constraints.'],task:'Survey va interview uchun integrated synthesis: convergence, difference va shared limitation.',model:'The survey favours a fixed afternoon, whereas interviews with distant students identify constraints on attending that day. These findings are not necessarily contradictory because the sources address different aspects of the decision. Taken together, they support testing a schedule that balances general preference with access needs. Neither source measures long-term attendance, so the recommendation remains provisional. A synthesis should explain how the evidence relates rather than select one source merely because it is larger or more detailed.',structure:['The sources address different questions.','Preference and constraints are both relevant.','Integrate them while retaining the shared limitation.']}),
brief('C1',19,{t:'Argument evaluation: premise va xulosa',goal:'Dalildan xulosaga o‘tishdagi yashirin farazni aniqlaysiz.',rules:'Valid inference premise bilan conclusion aloqasini, evidence esa premise rostligini tekshiradi.|Popularity effectivenessga, correlation causationga avtomatik dalil emas.|Counterexample qamrovni tekshiradi; qarshi fikrni xolis kuchli shaklda bayon qiling.',pattern:'premise → inference → conclusion / assumption → test',ex:['The argument assumes that attendance indicates benefit.','That premise needs independent support.','A popular programme is not necessarily effective.','The counterexample narrows the conclusion.','We should distinguish validity from truth.','The recommendation follows only if the assumption holds.'],v:'premise|asos faraz|/ˈpremɪs/|an unsupported premise;validity|mantiqiy to‘g‘rilik|/vəˈlɪdəti/|test validity;counterexample|qarshi misol|/ˈkaʊntərɪɡˌzɑːmpəl/|consider a counterexample;warrant|xulosaga asos|/ˈwɒrənt/|the inferential warrant',read:'The proposal argues that the club is popular, so it must improve learning. Its evidence supports popularity, but the conclusion requires an additional premise: participation must produce the intended learning benefit. That premise has not been tested. A counterexample is possible: a popular activity can be enjoyable without improving the target skill. This does not prove the club is ineffective. It shows that the argument is incomplete. The committee asks for a measure of what participants can do before and after the programme, with relevant comparison evidence. Evaluating an argument therefore involves neither automatic acceptance nor automatic rejection. It identifies what would make the inference stronger and which claims remain unsupported.',question:['Yashirin premise?','participation produces learning benefit','the club is unpopular','all fun is harmful','Popularity→learning uchun qo‘shimcha faraz.'],inference:['Counterexample nima isbotlaydi?','the inference needs support','the club is definitely ineffective','nobody enjoys it','Argument incomplete, opposite proof emas.'],keyword:'premise',responseKey:'warrant',response:'Ask what connects the popularity evidence to the learning conclusion.',fill:[['A popular programme is not necessarily ___.','effective'],['The premise needs independent ___.','support'],['The conclusion follows only ___ the assumption holds.','if']],fix:['Popularity proves learning, and questioning it proves failure.','Popularity does not establish learning, and questioning the inference does not prove failure.'],transform:['Assumptionni oching: Many attend, therefore learning improves.','The argument assumes that attendance indicates learning benefit.'],task:'Argument evaluation: premise, warrant, counterexample va kerakli dalilni yozing.',model:'The proposal establishes that the club is popular, but its learning conclusion depends on an untested premise. Participation may be enjoyable without producing the target skill. This counterexample shows that popularity alone is insufficient; it does not establish that the club is ineffective. I recommend measuring what participants can do before and after the programme and considering relevant comparison evidence. The aim is to strengthen the inference rather than replace an unsupported positive claim with an equally unsupported negative one.',structure:['Popularity is observed.','Learning benefit is assumed.','Test the link before accepting the conclusion.']}),
brief('C1',20,{t:'Academic essay: dalil va counterargument',goal:'Talabchan essayda pozitsiyani kvalifikatsiya va manba bilan rivojlantirasiz.',rules:'Thesis aniq va bahslanuvchi; har paragraph alohida sabab va dalil.|Counterargument eng kuchli shaklda, response uning asosli qismiga javob.|Synthesis conclusion yangi dalil kiritmasdan oldingi reasoningni yakunlaydi.',pattern:'qualified thesis → evidence → counterargument → response',ex:['A mixed approach is preferable under the current constraints.','The evidence supports access rather than a universal benefit.','A reasonable objection concerns staffing.','That objection limits the scale, not the value, of a trial.','The conclusion remains conditional on the review results.','No source establishes a permanent solution.'],v:'thesis|asosiy pozitsiya|/ˈθiːsɪs/|a qualified thesis;rebuttal|qarshi javob|/rɪˈbʌtəl/|a reasoned rebuttal;nuance|nozik farq|/ˈnjuːɑːns/|preserve nuance;substantiation|dalillash|/səbˌstænʃiˈeɪʃən/|seek substantiation',read:'An essay recommends a mixed schedule because the survey and interviews reveal different access needs. Its strongest counterargument concerns staffing: alternating days may make reliable supervision harder. The first draft dismisses this concern as resistance to change. The revision acknowledges the real constraint and limits the trial to days when staff are available. The thesis becomes more qualified but more defensible. An academic argument should be able to explain why its recommendation survives a serious objection and where it must change. Qualification is not indecision. It defines the conditions under which the position is supported. The final paragraph therefore recommends a bounded trial and a review, rather than claiming that a permanent solution has been proven.',question:['Eng kuchli counterargument?','reliable staffing','hatred of change','no access needs','Supervision constraint.'],inference:['Qualification nima qiladi?','defines support conditions','removes every position','proves permanent success','Shartlarini aniqlaydi.'],keyword:'thesis',responseKey:'qualification',response:'A defensible position states the conditions under which it holds.',fill:[['The thesis remains conditional ___ the results.','on'],['An objection ___ staffing. (concern)','concerns'],['No source ___ a permanent solution. (establish)','establishes']],fix:['Staffing concerns are only resistance to change.','Staffing concerns are a relevant constraint on the trial.'],transform:['Qualified thesis: A mixed approach is always best.','A mixed approach is preferable under the current constraints.'],task:'Argumentative essay: qualified thesis, ikki manba, serious counterargument va bounded conclusion.',model:'A mixed schedule is preferable under the current constraints because the survey and interviews reveal different access needs. However, the staffing objection is substantial: alternating days may reduce reliable supervision. That concern limits the scale of the trial rather than making access irrelevant. I propose using only days with confirmed staff and reviewing participation after two weeks. The evidence does not establish a permanent solution, but it supports a bounded experiment. A qualified thesis makes the conditions of support visible and explains how the recommendation responds to a serious counterargument.',structure:['Evidence supports mixed access needs.','Staffing restricts the feasible options.','Recommend a bounded trial with explicit conditions.']}),
brief('C1',21,{t:'Proposal: ehtiyoj, resurs va baholash',goal:'Amalga oshadigan proposalni maqsad va success criterialari bilan tuzasiz.',rules:'Proposal ehtiyoj, taklif, implementation, resurs, risk va evaluationni bog‘laydi.|Success criterion faqat ish bajarilishi emas, maqsadga mos natija.|Contingency va mas’ul shaxs ochiq; o‘lchanmagan natijani kafolatlamang.',pattern:'need → action → resources → risk → evaluation',ex:['The proposal addresses unequal access.','Implementation would begin with a limited trial.','The organiser would coordinate staffing.','Success would be assessed against participation criteria.','Should staffing fall short, the original schedule would remain.','The proposal does not guarantee a long-term effect.'],v:'objective|maqsad|/əbˈdʒektɪv/|a measurable objective;resource|resurs|/rɪˈsɔːs/|allocate resources;mitigation|xavfni kamaytirish|/ˌmɪtɪˈɡeɪʃən/|risk mitigation;milestone|bosqich natijasi|/ˈmaɪlstəʊn/|a review milestone',read:'A proposal promises to improve access by adding one session. It lists the activity but gives no way to judge whether access improves. The editor asks the team to identify which students currently face barriers and how their participation will be recorded. The proposal then names a coordinator, specifies existing staffing, and includes a contingency if supervision is unavailable. The trial’s success criterion compares participation among the relevant groups rather than counting sessions delivered. This does not require an elaborate prediction model. It requires alignment between the need, the action, and the evidence used to evaluate it. A proposal can be ambitious while remaining honest about what a short trial can demonstrate.',question:['Activity bilan success farqi?','a delivered session is not proof of improved access','they are always identical','success needs no criteria','Maqsadga mos natija kerak.'],inference:['Relevant groups nega?','the need concerns their barriers','all groups are identical','only total sessions matter','Need bilan evaluation alignment.'],keyword:'objective',responseKey:'evaluation',response:'Evaluate the stated need rather than only counting completed activities.',fill:[['Success would be assessed ___ criteria.','against'],['The organiser would ___ staffing. (coordinate)','coordinate'],['Should staffing ___ short, retain the schedule. (fall)','fall']],fix:['We delivered one session, so access definitely improved.','We delivered one session; improved access requires participation evidence.'],transform:['Contingency bilan: If staffing falls short, the original schedule will remain.','Should staffing fall short, the original schedule will remain.'],task:'Proposal yozing: need, action, coordinator, resource, risk va goal-linked evaluation.',model:'We propose one additional session to test whether access improves for students who currently face timetable barriers. The organiser would coordinate existing staff and record participation by the relevant groups. Success would be assessed against that objective, not merely the number of sessions delivered. Should staffing fall short, the original schedule would remain. After two weeks, the committee would compare participation and interview a small group about remaining barriers. The proposal is ambitious enough to test a useful change while limiting its claims to what the trial can demonstrate.',structure:['A barrier has been identified.','A trial action and coordinator are specified.','Judge success using evidence aligned with the need.']}),
brief('C1',22,{t:'Analytical report: pattern va limitation',goal:'Natijalarni tavsif, talqin va tavsiyaga aniq ajratasiz.',rules:'Description data nimani ko‘rsatdi; interpretation nega bo‘lishi mumkin; recommendation nima qilish.|Patternni individual exception bilan inkor yoki universal rule bilan kuchaytirmang.|Limitationni appendixga yashirmang: xulosa doirasiga ta’sir etsa yonida yozing.',pattern:'finding → interpretation → limitation → recommendation',ex:['Attendance increased during the trial.','The increase was concentrated in one group.','The pattern may reflect the revised timetable.','The report cannot separate that effect from promotion.','A second trial should compare those factors.','The recommendation is provisional rather than permanent.'],v:'pattern|qonuniyat|/ˈpætən/|identify a pattern;confound|aralash sabab|/kənˈfaʊnd/|a possible confound;provisional|vaqtinchalik|/prəˈvɪʒənəl/|a provisional recommendation;disaggregate|guruhlarga ajratmoq|/ˌdɪsˈæɡrɪɡeɪt/|disaggregate results',read:'Attendance increased during the trial, mainly among students already living near the school. The timetable changed at the same time as a promotional campaign. A report attributing the increase entirely to the timetable would ignore a possible confound. The writer separates the observed pattern from its interpretation and recommends another trial that records how participants heard about the sessions. She also disaggregates the total by the groups relevant to the access objective. The limitation appears beside the finding because it changes what can be inferred, rather than as a minor footnote. Analytical reporting is useful when it helps decision makers see both a pattern and the questions that pattern leaves unanswered.',question:['Confound nima?','promotion changed at the same time','attendance was never counted','all distant students came','Ikki o‘zgarish bir vaqtda.'],inference:['Disaggregate nega?','total hides who gained access','to make every figure larger','to remove limitations','Group-specific aim.'],keyword:'confound',responseKey:'provisional',response:'The recommendation remains provisional until competing explanations are tested.',fill:[['The increase was ___ in one group. (concentrate)','concentrated'],['The pattern may ___ the timetable. (reflect)','reflect'],['The report cannot ___ the effects. (separate)','separate']],fix:['The increase proves the timetable alone caused it.','The increase may reflect the timetable, but promotion changed at the same time.'],transform:['Limit qo‘shing: The timetable caused the increase.','The increase may reflect the timetable, but the effect of promotion is uncertain.'],task:'Analytical report: disaggregated finding, two interpretations, limitation va testable recommendation.',model:'Attendance increased during the trial, but the increase was concentrated among students living near the school. The timetable revision and promotional campaign occurred together, so the report cannot separate their effects. I recommend a second trial that records how participants heard about the sessions and compares participation by the groups relevant to the access objective. The recommendation is provisional. Presenting the limitation beside the finding makes clear why the total is encouraging but insufficient to establish that the timetable alone improved equitable access.',structure:['Attendance increased unevenly.','Timetable and promotion changed together.','Disaggregate results and test competing explanations.']}),
brief('C1',23,{t:'Seminar, debat va muzokara',goal:'Boshqa fikrni xolis qayta aytib, shartli kelishuvga olib kelasiz.',rules:'Avval opponentning maqsad va dalilini tekshirib paraphrase qiling.|Negotiation pozitsiyadan ehtiyojga o‘tadi: qaysi constraint va priority ortda?|Spontan clarification, concession va summary qaror shartlarini ko‘rsatadi.',pattern:'If I understand you correctly … / Could we agree provided that …?',ex:['If I understand you correctly, staffing is your priority.','That is a fair concern, although access also matters.','Could we agree provided that supervision is confirmed?','Let me distinguish the cost from the scheduling issue.','What evidence would change your position?','Our agreement is conditional on the review.'],v:'concession|kelishishga yaqin qism|/kənˈseʃən/|offer a concession;interest|asosiy ehtiyoj|/ˈɪntrəst/|identify shared interests;impasse|kelishuvsiz holat|/ˈɪmpɑːs/|resolve an impasse;conditional|shartli|/kənˈdɪʃənəl/|a conditional agreement',read:'Two committee members disagree about the trial. One demands a fixed day, while the other insists on flexibility. The discussion becomes more productive when a student asks what each position protects. The fixed-day supporter needs reliable staffing; the flexibility supporter wants distant students to participate. These interests are not identical, but they are not automatically incompatible. The group considers alternating only between two days with confirmed supervision. Before accepting, one member paraphrases the proposal and checks the review condition. This prevents a polite nod from being recorded as unconditional agreement. A successful negotiation need not eliminate every difference. It should identify a workable next step, the conditions of consent, and the evidence that will guide reconsideration.',question:['Pozitsiya ortidagi birinchi ehtiyoj?','reliable staffing','dislike of distant students','no trial ever','Fixed day staff reliability.'],inference:['Paraphrase nega kerak?','to verify conditional agreement','to silence disagreement','to prove permanent consent','Shartli assentni tekshirish.'],keyword:'interest',responseKey:'conditional',response:'Check the conditions before recording agreement.',fill:[['If I understand you ___, staffing matters.','correctly'],['Could we agree ___ that supervision is confirmed?','provided'],['Our agreement is conditional ___ review.','on']],fix:['A polite nod proves unconditional agreement.','A polite nod should be checked before being recorded as unconditional agreement.'],transform:['Clarification so‘rang: Your priority seems to be staffing.','If I understand you correctly, staffing is your priority.'],task:'Role-play muzokara: ikki conflicting interest, concession, conditional agreement va review.',model:'The fixed-day position protects reliable staffing, while the flexible position protects access for distant students. I suggest alternating between two days only when supervision is confirmed. Before recording agreement, we should ask each member to state whether those conditions match their understanding. The trial would be reviewed using participation evidence after two weeks. This is a conditional next step rather than a permanent settlement. Negotiation becomes more productive when participants identify the needs behind their positions and verify consent instead of interpreting politeness as agreement.',structure:['Positions appear incompatible.','Underlying interests concern staffing and access.','Verify a workable conditional agreement.']}),
brief('C1',24,{t:'C1 loyiha: manbadan taqdimotgacha',goal:'Talabchan materialni synthesis, proposal va spontan savol-javobga aylantirasiz.',rules:'Synthesisda har manba claim, method va limitationini saqlang.|Proposal xulosa bilan actionni bog‘lasin; speakingda e’tirozga yangi, relevant javob bering.|C1 ichki loyiha natijasi rasmiy sertifikat emas; to‘rt ko‘nikma uchun rubrika dalili kerak.',pattern:'analyse → synthesise → propose → defend → revise',ex:['The sources converge on the importance of access.','They diverge on how demand was measured.','The proposal remains conditional on staffing.','A plausible objection concerns selection bias.','In response, I would broaden the sample.','The final recommendation should reflect that revision.'],v:'convergence|mos dalillar|/kənˈvɜːdʒəns/|identify convergence;selection bias|tanlov og‘ishi|/sɪˈlekʃən baɪəs/|address selection bias;defensible|asoslanadigan|/dɪˈfensəbəl/|a defensible recommendation;revision|qayta ishlash|/rɪˈvɪʒən/|evidence-based revision',read:'Source A reports a survey of sixty students, most of whom prefer a fixed afternoon. Respondents completed it voluntarily through a link shared in the school group. The report treats preference as evidence of demand, but it does not show whether non-respondents face different constraints. Source B presents six interviews with students living far from the school. They describe transport difficulties and favour rotating days. These interviews explain a barrier but cannot estimate how common it is across the school. Source C records staffing availability. Two afternoons have reliable supervision, although one depends on a teacher who may leave next term. Taken together, the sources support a bounded trial on those two days, not a permanent claim that every group will benefit. The evaluation should record participation by relevant groups, distinguish attendance from active engagement, and ask how students heard about the sessions. A presenter must also explain what evidence would change the recommendation. If distant students still cannot attend, rotation alone may not address the barrier. If staffing becomes unreliable, the proposal needs revision even if demand is strong. A defensible decision links evidence, conditions, and willingness to reconsider rather than presenting confidence as proof.',question:['B intervyularining cheklovi?','they cannot estimate school-wide prevalence','they contain no barriers','they have sixty respondents','Olti kishidan frequency umumlashmaydi.'],inference:['Final tavsiya qanday?','a bounded trial with conditions and review','a permanent guarantee','a decision based only on confidence','Har manba limitini saqlaydi.'],keyword:'selection bias',responseKey:'revision',response:'A defensible recommendation identifies what evidence would require revision.',fill:[['The sources ___ on the importance of access. (converge)','converge'],['The proposal is conditional ___ staffing.','on'],['The recommendation should ___ the revision. (reflect)','reflect']],fix:['A confident presentation proves that every source is reliable.','A confident presentation does not establish that every source is reliable.'],transform:['Conditional proposal: We can trial both days if staffing is confirmed.','We can trial both days provided that staffing is confirmed.'],task:'C1 yakuniy loyiha: uch manba synthesisidan 180–250 so‘zli proposal yozing. 2–3 daqiqali taqdimot qiling va selection bias hamda staffing haqida spontan savolga javob bering.',minWords:180,model:'We recommend a bounded trial alternating between the two afternoons with confirmed supervision. The sources converge on the importance of access, but they measure different things. The voluntary survey records preference among respondents, while six interviews explain transport constraints for a particular group. Neither establishes how common every barrier is across the school. Staffing records identify feasible days, although one arrangement may change next term.\n\nThe trial should therefore collect participation data by relevant groups rather than rely on a total alone. It should also ask how students heard about the sessions, since promotion could affect attendance independently of the timetable. Active engagement should be assessed separately from presence. The organiser would coordinate staffing and publish a review date before the trial begins.\n\nA reasonable objection concerns selection bias in the survey. We would address it by inviting responses through more than one route and checking which groups remain underrepresented. Should supervision become unreliable, the original timetable would remain. If distant students still cannot attend, rotation alone may be insufficient. The recommendation is defensible because it names its evidence, conditions, and grounds for revision rather than claiming a permanent solution.',structure:['The sources measure different aspects of access.','A trial can test a limited combined response.','Review evidence and revise when the conditions fail.'],speak:'Uch manbani qiyoslang, proposalni 2–3 daqiqada himoya qiling, keyin sherikning kutilmagan savoliga javob bering.'})
];
